---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { articles, articlePath } from '../content/articles.mjs';
import { SITE_ORIGIN } from '../lib/site.mjs';

// Developer entry point. is-agentic requires a real /developers page linked from
// the homepage, with actual jobs, requests and limitations rather than a stub.
const sampleSlug = articles[0]?.slug || 'what-agent-ready-actually-means';

const techArticle = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'TechArticle',
	headline: 'emin.de developer documentation',
	description: 'Public JSON API, Streamable HTTP MCP server and one x402-paid bulk endpoint for the emin.de article corpus.',
	url: `${SITE_ORIGIN}/developers`,
	author: { '@id': `${SITE_ORIGIN}/#person` },
	publisher: { '@id': `${SITE_ORIGIN}/#organization` },
};
---

<Base
	title="Developers: API, MCP and x402 | emin.de"
	description="Public JSON API, a Streamable HTTP MCP server with five read-only tools, and one x402-paid bulk corpus endpoint. Copy-paste curl for every surface, no credentials needed except for bulk access."
	path="/developers"
	structuredData={techArticle}
	markdownPath="/developers/index.md"
>
	<h1>Developers</h1>

	<div class="tldr">
		<strong>TL;DR</strong>
		<p>
			Every article here is available four ways: as HTML, as Markdown at a predictable URL, as JSON over a public
			API, and through an MCP server. None of those need credentials. One endpoint is paid: the full corpus in a
			single call, settled with x402.
		</p>
	</div>

	<h2>What you can actually do</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>List every article with metadata, to decide what to fetch.</li>
		<li>Fetch one article as clean Markdown, with no navigation, footer or cookie banner in the way.</li>
		<li>Search article text by keyword.</li>
		<li>Pull the entire corpus in one request, if you would rather pay once than crawl.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>What you cannot do, so you do not waste a call</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>There are no user accounts, so there is no authentication to obtain for the free surfaces.</li>
		<li>There is no write operation of any kind. Every endpoint is read-only.</li>
		<li>There is no private or personal data behind any endpoint.</li>
		<li>There is no search beyond the article corpus. This is not a web index.</li>
	</ul>

	<h2>Markdown mirrors</h2>
	<p>Append <code>/index.md</code> to any page URL, or ask for Markdown with an Accept header on the page URL itself.</p>
	<pre><code>curl -s {SITE_ORIGIN}/posts/{sampleSlug}/index.md

# or negotiate on the HTML URL
curl -s -H 'Accept: text/markdown' {SITE_ORIGIN}/posts/{sampleSlug}</code></pre>
	<p>
		Responses carry <code>Vary: Accept</code>, so a cache cannot serve the HTML representation to a client that asked
		for Markdown.
	</p>

	<h2>JSON API</h2>
	<p>Typed schemas for every operation are in <a href="/openapi.json">openapi.json</a>. Errors use RFC 9457 problem details.</p>
	<pre><code># every article, with metadata
curl -s {SITE_ORIGIN}/api/articles

# one article, including its full Markdown body
curl -s {SITE_ORIGIN}/api/articles/{sampleSlug}

# an unknown slug returns application/problem+json, status 404
curl -s -i {SITE_ORIGIN}/api/articles/no-such-article</code></pre>

	<h2>MCP server</h2>
	<p>
		Streamable HTTP at <code>/mcp</code>, read-only, no credentials. The server card at
		<a href="/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json">/.well-known/mcp/server-card.json</a> lists the transport and the
		tools, and the tool list there matches what the server actually implements:
		<code>list_articles</code>, <code>get_article_markdown</code>, <code>search_articles</code>,
		<code>get_site_summary</code>, <code>list_topics</code>.
	</p>
	<pre><code># initialize, then list tools
curl -s -X POST {SITE_ORIGIN}/mcp \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '&lbrace;"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":1,"method":"initialize","params":&lbrace;"protocolVersion":"2025-06-18","capabilities":&lbrace;&rbrace;,"clientInfo":&lbrace;"name":"curl","version":"1.0"&rbrace;&rbrace;&rbrace;'

curl -s -X POST {SITE_ORIGIN}/mcp \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '&lbrace;"jsonrpc":"2.0","id":2,"method":"tools/list"&rbrace;'</code></pre>

	<h2>Paid bulk access with x402</h2>
	<p>
		<code>GET /api/v1/corpus</code> returns every article's complete Markdown in one response. An unpaid request
		returns a real HTTP 402 with a machine-readable challenge stating the amount, scheme, network and recipient. The
		exact values in the challenge match what <a href="/openapi.json">openapi.json</a> declares.
	</p>
	<pre><code># see the challenge
curl -s -i {SITE_ORIGIN}/api/v1/corpus</code></pre>
	<p>
		This endpoint exists because crawling is the wrong shape for bulk ingestion, not as a paywall: every article is
		free to read individually, as HTML, as Markdown and over the JSON API. You are paying for one call instead of
		many, and you are not required to.
	</p>

	<h2>Rate limits and deprecation</h2>
	<p>
		API paths carry IETF <code>RateLimit</code> and <code>RateLimit-Policy</code> headers, and an exceeded limit
		returns 429 with <code>Retry-After</code>. The limits in those headers are the limits actually enforced.
	</p>
	<p>
		If an endpoint is ever retired it will first return the <code>Deprecation</code> header (RFC 9745) and a
		<code>Sunset</code> header (RFC 8594) with the removal date, and be marked <code>deprecated: true</code> in the
		OpenAPI document. Nothing is removed without that sequence.
	</p>

	<h2>Terms</h2>
	<p>
		Article text is licensed CC BY 4.0: quote it, translate it, train on it, with attribution to emin.de. The site's
		own code is MIT and the full source is <a href="https://github.com/eminogrande/emin-de">on GitHub</a>, including
		the implementation of every endpoint on this page.
	</p>
	<p>
		Authentication notes: <a href="/auth.md">auth.md</a>. Discovery map: <a href="/llms.txt">llms.txt</a>.
	</p>
</Base>
